\section{Shared conventions}{

\itemize{
  \item \code{conf_level} sets every interval in the tables the function
    returns. The one thing it does not reach is \code{$emmeans_object},
    which is \pkg{emmeans}' own grid and carries \pkg{emmeans}' default
    level; pass \code{level =} yourself when you summarise it.
  \item \code{plots = TRUE} builds \pkg{ggplot2} objects and returns them;
    nothing is ever drawn as a side effect.
  \item \code{verbose = FALSE} by default; progress goes through
    \code{\link[base]{message}}, never \code{\link[base]{cat}}. No analysis
    function writes to the console: warnings and messages raised by
    \pkg{car}, \code{\link[stats]{glm}} and \pkg{afex} are captured into
    \code{$notes} instead.
  \item Rows with missing or infinite values in the analysed columns are
    dropped. \code{$n_removed} counts the input rows that are not in
    \code{$data_used}, so \code{nrow($data_used) + $n_removed} is always the
    number of rows supplied.
  \item Grouping columns are coerced to factors and unused level combinations
    are dropped, so the number of groups reported is the number that contain
    data.
  \item \code{posthoc = FALSE} skips the pairwise comparisons, which cost
    \code{choose(k, 2)} tests, and says so in \code{$notes}.
  \item Prior weights, where a function accepts them, are named as a column
    rather than passed as a vector, so they are subsetted with the data.
  \item Anything the function decided on your behalf, or could not compute,
    is recorded in \code{$notes}. Read it.
}
}

\section{Multiplicity adjustments}{

\code{adjust} takes different values depending on where the comparisons come
from. \code{\link{anova_welch}} and \code{\link{anova_kw}} compare directly
and accept the \code{\link[stats]{p.adjust}} methods (\code{"holm"},
\code{"hochberg"}, \code{"hommel"}, \code{"bonferroni"}, \code{"BH"},
\code{"BY"}, \code{"fdr"}, \code{"none"}), defaulting to \code{"holm"} and
\code{"BH"} respectively. The other six go through \pkg{emmeans} and
additionally accept \code{"tukey"} (their default), \code{"sidak"},
\code{"scheffe"} and \code{"dunnettx"}.
}

